<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kamień Pomorski</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Cammin</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kamień Pomorski, in German Cammin, is a town near the mouth of the Oder in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. From the late 12th century it was the seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania">Pomerania</a>, whose Romanesque-Gothic cathedral survives. The bishopric's lands passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> after the Peace of Westphalia, and the town became Polish in 1945.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kamien-pomorski.jpg" alt="Kamień Pomorski" width="320" height="202" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ralf Roletschek · CC BY-SA 1.0 fi · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:20-08-09-Kamien-Pomorskie-RalfR-101-0206-Pano.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Kamień Pomorski lies on the Dziwna strait, where it forms the Kamieński Bay, on the Baltic coast of the West Pomeranian Voivodeship, about 63 km north of the regional capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/szczecin">Szczecin</a>. The centre of the town is about 7 km from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baltic-sea">Baltic Sea</a>, to which it has direct access. Its name means Pomeranian Stone and is Slavic in origin. It is associated with a glacial boulder about 20 m across in the bed of the Dziwna, known as the Royal Boulder, which was used as a marker for ships and has been a protected natural monument since 1959. Local legends tell that Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boleslaw-iii-wrymouth">Bolesław III Wrymouth</a> stood on it in 1121 to greet sailors, and give it other, supernatural origins.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The town was founded at the turn of the 8th and 9th centuries by the Lechitic Wolinians tribe and became part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> soon after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mieszko-i">Mieszko I</a> created the state, around 967. A stronghold was probably built there in the 10th century, and the town is first mentioned in documents in 1124. After the fragmentation of Poland in the 12th century it belonged to the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania">Pomerania</a> and was the duchy's first known capital. A bishopric was seated there in 1176, a mint was established in 1180, and the Dominicans were active in the town's religious life by 1228. It received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a> city rights in 1274.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> acquired control of the town at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-westphalia">Peace of Westphalia</a> in 1648, although it remained part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>. It became part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Kingdom of Prussia</a> in 1701 and was administered within the Prussian Province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania">Pomerania</a>. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> the Germans ran a forced labour subcamp of the Stalag II-D prisoner-of-war camp there. In 1945 the town returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> under the border changes agreed at the Potsdam Conference. A hoard of early medieval jewellery was found in excavations in the town between 1959 and 1961.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Kamień Pomorski is a spa town and the seat of an urban-rural gmina in Kamień County. It is the second seat of the Archdiocese of Szczecin-Kamień. The Cathedral of St. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-the-baptist">John the Baptist</a> is a local landmark and has been one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>'s official national Historic Monuments since 1 September 2005. The town was the birthplace of Ewald Georg von Kleist, the jurist, cleric and physicist who invented the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leyden-jar">Leyden jar</a>, and of the writer Uwe Johnson.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kamień Pomorski” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A bishopric for Pomerania was founded at Wolin in 1140 and moved to Kamień (<b>Cammin</b>) in the 1170s.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-pomerania">Duchy of Pomerania · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The bishopric of Pomerania was founded in 1140 at Wolin after the mission of Otto of Bamberg, with Adalbert as first bishop, and moved to <b>Cammin</b> by 1176.</blockquote><a href="/state/prince-bishopric-of-cammin">Prince-Bishopric of Cammin</a></li></ul></section>
